## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model simple state transitions and undo/redo semantics with basic data structures.

## Key Concepts

- History management with stacks
- Amortized O(1) operations
- Edge cases: empty history, initial page

## Code Example

```javascript
class BrowserHistory {
  constructor(startUrl) {
    this.back = [];
    this.forward = [];
    this.currentUrl = startUrl ?? null;
  }
  visit(url) {
    if (this.currentUrl !== null) this.back.push(this.currentUrl);
    this.forward = [];
    this.currentUrl = url;
  }
  back(steps) {
    while (steps > 0 && this.back.length) {
      this.forward.push(this.currentUrl);
      this.currentUrl = this.back.pop();
      steps--;
    }
    return this.currentUrl;
  }
  forward(steps) {
    while (steps > 0 && this.forward.length) {
      this.back.push(this.currentUrl);
      this.currentUrl = this.forward.pop();
      steps--;
    }
    return this.currentUrl;
  }
  current() {
    return this.currentUrl;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale to multiple tabs with independent histories?
- How would you extend to persist history across sessions without refetching pages?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a scalable data structure for tracking peak occupancy across a large domain with many overlapping intervals—a common problem in campaign management, resource allocation, and scheduling systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Implicit segment tree**: Enables efficient operations over a massive coordinate space (up to 2^31-1) without building a full tree
- **Range add, range max with argmax**: Combines lazy propagation for updates with maintaining both value and position of the maximum
- **Incremental updates**: Insert and erase operations dynamically modify the tree state
- **Handling duplicates**: Multiple intervals covering the same coordinate naturally accumulate via additive weights

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor() {
    this.max = 0;
    this.argMax = 0;
    this.lazy = 0;
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
  }
}

function rangeAdd(node, L, R, ql, qr, delta) {
  if (qr < L || R < ql) return;
  if (ql <= L && R <= qr) {
    node.max += delta;
    node.lazy += delta;
    return;
  }
  push(node);
  const mid = (L + R) >>> 1;
  rangeAdd(node.left, L, mid, ql, qr, delta);
  rangeAdd(node.right, mid + 1, R, ql, qr, delta);
  pull(node);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle coordinate compression for non-uniform distributions?
- What are the time complexities for insert, erase, and query operations?
- How would you extend this to support minimum queries or range sum queries?

## Complexity Analysis
- **Time**: O(log N) per insertInterval, eraseInterval, and queryGlobalMax, where N is the coordinate domain size (2^31)
- **Space**: O(k log N) where k is the number of active intervals, since only nodes touched by operations are created (sparse representation)

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a practical data structure and reason about block balancing in edit-heavy scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- **Sqrt decomposition**: Partition data into fixed-size blocks for efficient range queries
- **Per-block sums**: Enable O(1) block-level aggregation during rangeSum operations
- **Amortized balance**: Split/merge operations maintain block sizes within bounds, ensuring O(√n) amortized time per update
- **Edge-case handling**: Proper merge/split logic prevents pathological degradation

## Code Example
```javascript
class SqrtArray {
  constructor() {
    this.blocks = [];
    this.B = 2;
  }

  _getBlock(index) {
    let count = 0;
    for (const block of this.blocks) {
      if (index < count + block.length) return block;
      count += block.length;
    }
    throw new Error('Index out of bounds');
  }

  insertAt(index, value) {
    const block = this._getBlock(index);
    block.push(value);
    block.sum += value;
    if (block.length > 2 * this.B) this._split(block);
  }

  eraseAt(index) {
    const block = this._getBlock(index);
    block.splice(index, 1);
    block.sum -= value;
    if (block.length < this.B / 2) this._merge(block);
  }

  rangeSum(l, r) {
    let sum = 0;
    let count = 0;
    for (const block of this.blocks) {
      const blockStart = count;
      const blockEnd = count + block.length - 1;
      if (r < blockStart || l > blockEnd) { count += block.length; continue; }
      const start = Math.max(l, blockStart);
      const end = Math.min(r, blockEnd);
      if (start === blockStart && end === blockEnd) sum += block.sum;
      else for (let i = start; i <= end; i++) sum += block[i];
      count += block.length;
    }
    return sum;
  }

  _split(block) {
    const mid = Math.floor(block.length / 2);
    const newBlock = { arr: block.arr.splice(mid), sum: newBlock.arr.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0) };
    block.sum = block.arr.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
    const idx = this.blocks.indexOf(block);
    this.blocks.splice(idx + 1, 0, newBlock);
  }

  _merge(block) {
    const idx = this.blocks.indexOf(block);
    const neighbor = idx > 0 ? this.blocks[idx - 1] : this.blocks[idx + 1];
    const [small, big] = neighbor.length > block.length ? [block, neighbor] : [neighbor, block];
    big.arr.push(...small.arr);
    big.sum += small.sum;
    this.blocks.splice(this.blocks.indexOf(small), 1);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of rope-like data structures for dynamic strings, enabling efficient insert/delete operations alongside range hashing capabilities.

## Key Concepts

- Implicit treap (rope) as a dynamic string container
- Node fields: character, subtree size, rolling hash
- Split and merge operations maintain size and hash invariants
- Rolling hash with precomputed powers and modulo arithmetic for collision reduction

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch: node structure with split/merge operations
class Node {
  constructor(ch) {
    this.ch = ch;
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
    this.size = 1;
    this.hash = ch.charCodeAt(0);
  }
}
```

